    <DataNode elementId="AT5G54190-MONOMER" textLabel="Protochlorophyllide reductase A, chloroplastic" type="Protein">
      <Xref identifier="Q42536" dataSource="uniprot" />
      <Graphics centerX="150" centerY="215" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="000000" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="000000" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="pmngroup">The NADPH:protochlorophyllide oxidoreductase (POR) catalyzes the photoreduction of protochlorophyllide to chlorophyllide in higher plants. It has been shown that there are two POR&apos;s active, POR A which is exclusively expressed in young plants and is required for greening and POR B which is expressed both in seedlings and adult plants and sustains light-dependent chlorophyll biosynthesis in mature plants |CITS: [7659751]| |CITS: [8624514]|. Although a wealth of information has been accumulated on the functions and properties of the light-dependent PORA and PORB enzymes, the only functional information available at the time of this review concern PORC.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="AT5G54190-MONOMER" />
